AB  - Instructors of large programming courses see students' final submissions but rarely the process that produced them. We built SessionReel, a tool that reconstructs a student's editing session from keystroke logs and plays it back at adjustable speed with run and error markers. In a within-subjects study, 9 instructors and 14 teaching assistants examined 40 sessions and were asked to locate the moment a student became stuck. With final code alone, participants located the stuck point within 60 seconds in 41% of sessions; with replay, 77%. Replay cost a median 3.8 minutes per session. We report which visual markers helped and argue that replay is most useful as a targeted tool for sessions already flagged by simpler signals.
