CY  - Charlottesville, VA, USA
AB  - Studies that label novice programmers' behavioral states from logs choose a window length with little justification. We asked three trained raters to label the same 400 minutes of replayed programming sessions at three window lengths (10, 30 and 60 seconds) using a five-state scheme. Agreement was highest for 30-second windows (Fleiss' kappa 0.66), lower for 60 seconds (0.61), where windows often spanned two states, and lowest for 10 seconds (0.48), where raters lacked context. Most disagreements fell between "tinkering" and "stuck". We release our rater training materials and recommend 30-second windows with a one-window context margin.
LA  - en
KW  - labeling
KW  - inter-rater reliability
KW  - learner states
