AB  - We interviewed 22 CS1 students about how they understand and use an AI code assistant permitted in their course. Students held three recurring models of the assistant: an oracle that knows the answer, a rubber duck that helps them think, and a shortcut that saves time on parts they consider unimportant. Models shifted within a single session, often after a failed run. Students who described the assistant as a rubber duck reported asking later and asking narrower questions. We discuss implications for when and how assistants should respond to novices.
